<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomb of the Lictor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tomba del Littore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tomb of the Lictor (Tomba del Littore) is an Etruscan circle grave of the 7th century BCE at Vetulonia in Tuscany, excavated by Isidoro Falchi in the late 19th century. Among its finds was an iron double-headed axe set in a bundle of iron rods, taken as an early example of the fasces, the symbol of authority that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> claimed to have borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>.</p></div>
